@page
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}

<div>
    <h1 class="display-4">About GCF:</h1>

    <H2>Rules</H2>
    <ol>
        <li>
            Each month has 6-8 active games + 1 long-term slot + 1 completion slot.
        </li>

        <li>
            1 centerpiece game (main focus).
        </li>

        <li>
            Roll 3 tags per month to guide selection; reroll any uninspiring ones.
        </li>

        <li>
            Limit 1–3 new games per month to avoid obligation burnout.
        </li>

        <li>
            Fatigue Clause: if a game feels draining, rotate or replace it guilt-free.
        </li>

        <li>
            Near-Finish Rule: short “wrap-up” games go in the completion slot.
        </li>

        <li>
            Rollover Rule: unfinished but enjoyable games can carry to next month.
        </li>

        <li>
            Wildcard Slot (optional): 1 extra game for sudden interest or bundle pick.
        </li>

        <li>
            No heavy or same themes back to back
        </li>

        <li>
            Weekday Rule: Ensure 2-3 games with short loops, low ramp-up cost, and clean stopping points.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <H2>What is the GCF or Game Curation Framework?</H2>

    <p>
        The Game Curation Framework started as an experiment to solve choice paralysis.
    </p>
    <p>
        I came across a video discussing how large game libraries can become overwhelming. Their solution was simple: pick 12 games, one for each month, and focus exclusively on that game until you either finished it or decided it wasn't for you.
    </p>
    <p>
        The idea made sense, but I quickly realized it wouldn't work for me. I like variety. Playing only one game for an entire month felt restrictive rather than freeing.
    </p>
    <p>
        The first version of the framework was October Horror Month. I picked a list of 10 horror games and committed to exploring a genre I rarely touched. It worked surprisingly well. I finished a game, experienced something I normally would have skipped, and immediately ran into a new problem: after finishing a horror game, I didn't necessarily want another horror game.
    </p>
    <p>
        That led to monthly tags. Instead of a single theme, each month would roll three tags to create a varied pool of games. To keep the system from becoming a prison, I gave myself unlimited vetoes and rerolls. The goal was inspiration, not obligation.

        <p>
            As I used the system, new problems kept appearing. Consecutive months of dark or heavy games caused burnout, so I added rules to avoid repeating themes back-to-back. Good sales and Humble Bundle pickups didn't always fit the month's tags, so the Wildcard slot was created. A frustrating experience with No More Heroes 3 led to the Fatigue Clause, allowing games to be rotated out guilt-free if they stopped being fun.
        </p>
        <p>
            Eventually I encountered a different form of paralysis. I would finish a game and hesitate to start another because every remaining option was a brand-new commitment. To solve that, I introduced ongoing slots for long-term games, near-finished games, and games already in progress. Later, the Rollover Rule appeared for a simple reason: if I was genuinely enjoying a game at the end of the month, why stop?
        </p>
        <p>
            The framework wasn't designed all at once. It evolved as solutions to problems I actually encountered while using it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Today, the framework's purpose isn't to finish a backlog. It's to make choosing games easier, encourage variety, and create opportunities to experience games I might otherwise ignore. Since starting it, I've played more genres, discovered more unexpected favorites, and spent far less time staring at my library wondering what to play.
        </p>
    </div>
